V3DRender 0.08: kit 8, the V3D's power
=======================================

Kit 7: Mesa's Z store hangs into any memory (its own buffer, the colour
buffer's, a new one), while the colour store works into the Z buffer's
memory. So the memory is fine and storing Z itself never finishes. The
same job runs on Linux, so something about how the GPU was started
differs. Linux has the Pi's firmware power the V3D (its power domain),
and a full power-up includes steps such as the repair of the GPU's own
RAMs (its tile buffers among them). V3DRender only ever started its
clock and pulsed its reset.

V3DRender 0.08 asks the firmware for the power domain at load (when
the GPU isn't already running), and adds *V3DPower: it shows the
domain and the power register, and *V3DPower cycle has the firmware
turn the domain off and on.

Replay now runs the job (and its changes) once as things are, then
*V3DPower cycle, then again. If the Z store works after the cycle,
that was it.

If the machine freezes at the cycle, restart and say so.

Before you start
- A Pi 4, Pi 400 or CM4 with RISC OS 5.30 or later. V3DDriver not
  loaded. Copy this folder to a hard disc.

The test
1. Double-click Replay. It loads V3DRender 0.08 from this folder, in
   place of an older one. No program should be using the GPU at the same time:
   Replay needs the buffer addresses Mesa's job had.
2. Optional: double-click Check too (kits 1-5's tests, all passed in
   kit 5).
3. Send Results back.
